   "creator_full": "Daily History Clips | DHC",
   "caption": "Glenn Gould was one of the great pianists of the twentieth century, and he sang while he played. Not quietly. A wordless humming that followed whatever line his hands were on, and it is there on almost every record he made.\n\nColumbia\u2019s engineers spent years trying to get rid of it. They pulled the microphones further from his face, changed the angles, tried putting a screen between him and the keyboard. Nothing worked. There was nowhere to put a microphone that could hear the piano but not the man bent over it.\n\nGould never treated it as a fault. He said he was not so much playing the music as thinking it, and the voice was part of the thinking.\n\nHe traced the habit back to his childhood, to an afternoon when the family vacuum cleaner was running while he practised. The noise swallowed the piano completely, and he realised he could hear the piece more clearly in his head than through his ears. He learned everything that way afterwards, away from the instrument, so by the time he sat down the music was already sounding inside him. The humming was just the overflow.\n\nPut on his Goldberg Variations and there is a second instrument in the room, slightly out of tune with the first.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Join the community @dailyhistoryclips \n\n#GlennGould #classicalmusic #piano #1960s #recordingstudio",
